WEBVTT

00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:18.876
<v Host>Welcome to Japanese Daily News. I'm your host. Today is May twenty-fourth. This is our very first episode. Today's story: more foreigners are visiting Japan than ever before. The number just passed thirty million. Let's listen.

00:00:19.475 --> 00:00:27.012
<v News>去年、日本に来た外国人の数が3000万人を超えました。

00:00:27.711 --> 00:00:36.832
<v News>一番多い国は中国です。次は韓国、そしてアメリカです。

00:00:37.531 --> 00:00:45.283
<v News>外国人に人気がある場所は、東京、大阪、京都です。

00:00:45.984 --> 00:00:52.944
<v News>最近は、北海道や沖縄に行く人も増えています。

00:00:53.643 --> 00:01:00.844
<v News>外国人がたくさん来るので、ホテルの値段が高くなっています。

00:01:01.543 --> 00:01:13.664
<v News>日本語が分からなくて困る外国人もいます。駅やレストランで、英語のメニューや案内が増えています。

00:01:14.364 --> 00:01:22.212
<v News>日本政府は、「もっと多くの外国人に来てほしい」と言っています。

00:01:22.912 --> 00:01:30.328
<v Host>Okay. This story uses simple, everyday Japanese. Perfect for beginners. Let's break it down.

00:01:30.927 --> 00:01:32.703
<v Host>The first sentence:

00:01:33.304 --> 00:01:46.528
<v Slow>Exceeded, passed. When a number goes over a target, you use this verb.

00:01:47.328 --> 00:01:52.871
<v Host>Last year, the number of foreigners who came to Japan exceeded thirty million.

00:01:53.471 --> 00:01:55.487
<v Vocab>去年

00:01:55.888 --> 00:02:03.447
<v Host>Last year. One of the first time words you learn. This year is 'kotoshi,' and next year is 'rainen.'

00:02:04.048 --> 00:02:06.664
<v Vocab>外国人

00:02:07.063 --> 00:02:13.855
<v Host>Foreigner. The three characters mean outside, country, person. Very logical.

00:02:14.455 --> 00:02:16.376
<v Vocab>人

00:02:16.776 --> 00:02:23.448
<v Host>It means person. So the word literally means outside-country-person. Very logical.

00:02:24.048 --> 00:02:26.207
<v Vocab>超える

00:02:26.608 --> 00:02:30.183
<v Host>When a number goes over a target, you use this verb.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.247
<v Host>Where are they from?

00:02:32.848 --> 00:02:44.223
<v Slow>一番多い国は中国です。次は韓国、そしてアメリカです。

00:02:45.024 --> 00:02:53.544
<v Host>The country with the most visitors is China. Next is South Korea, then America. 'Ichiban' means number one, the most.

00:02:54.144 --> 00:02:56.304
<v Vocab>多い

00:02:56.704 --> 00:03:02.127
<v Host>It means many. Together with 'ichiban,' it means the most numerous.

00:03:02.728 --> 00:03:04.479
<v Vocab>次

00:03:04.879 --> 00:03:09.199
<v Host>Next. Short and useful. You'll hear it everywhere.

00:03:09.800 --> 00:03:11.647
<v Host>What places are popular?

00:03:12.247 --> 00:03:21.943
<v Slow>外国人に人気がある場所は、東京、大阪、京都です。

00:03:22.743 --> 00:03:27.759
<v Host>The places popular with foreigners are Tokyo, Osaka, and Kyoto.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:31.024
<v Vocab>人気がある

00:03:31.424 --> 00:03:37.639
<v Host>Popular. Literally: has people's spirit. The first part means popularity.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.352
<v Vocab>がある

00:03:40.752 --> 00:03:46.080
<v Host>It means 'it exists.' Together: is popular. For example:

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:51.288
<v Slow>この店は人気があります。

00:03:52.087 --> 00:03:56.312
<v Host>This shop is popular. You can use this pattern for anything.

00:03:56.912 --> 00:03:59.000
<v Vocab>場所

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:03.743
<v Host>Place, location. A basic but very important word.

00:04:04.343 --> 00:04:05.855
<v Host>And new trends:

00:04:06.455 --> 00:04:15.144
<v Slow>最近は、北海道や沖縄に行く人も増えています。

00:04:15.943 --> 00:04:21.752
<v Host>Recently, the number of people going to Hokkaido and Okinawa is also increasing.

00:04:22.351 --> 00:04:24.704
<v Vocab>最近

00:04:25.103 --> 00:04:30.288
<v Host>Recently. Use it at the beginning of a sentence to talk about current trends.

00:04:30.887 --> 00:04:33.144
<v Vocab>ています

00:04:33.543 --> 00:04:41.944
<v Host>This form means it's an ongoing change — something is increasing right now and continues to increase. You'll hear it a lot in news.

00:04:42.543 --> 00:04:44.175
<v Host>There's a side effect:

00:04:44.776 --> 00:04:53.776
<v Slow>外国人がたくさん来るので、ホテルの値段が高くなっています。

00:04:54.576 --> 00:05:11.615
<v Host>Because many foreigners are coming, hotel prices have gone up. 'Takusan' means a lot, many — very basic, very useful. 'Nedan' means price. 'Nedan ga takai' means the price is high. 'Nedan ga yasui' means it's cheap.

00:05:12.216 --> 00:05:14.160
<v Host>Notice the grammar here:

00:05:14.759 --> 00:05:16.704
<v Vocab>ので

00:05:17.103 --> 00:05:24.519
<v Host>It means 'because.' It connects the reason and the result. Because foreigners come, prices go up.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:26.752
<v Host>And a challenge:

00:05:27.351 --> 00:05:34.216
<v Slow>日本語が分からなくて困る外国人もいます。

00:05:35.016 --> 00:05:39.240
<v Host>There are also foreigners who don't understand Japanese and have trouble.

00:05:39.839 --> 00:05:42.048
<v Vocab>分かる

00:05:42.447 --> 00:05:46.672
<v Host>to understand. One of the most important verbs in Japanese.

00:05:47.271 --> 00:05:49.360
<v Vocab>困る

00:05:49.759 --> 00:05:58.831
<v Host>To be in trouble, to have difficulty. When you're lost in Japan and can't read the signs, that feeling is 'komaru.' The solution:

00:05:59.432 --> 00:06:07.879
<v Slow>駅やレストランで、英語のメニューや案内が増えています。

00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:13.911
<v Host>At stations and restaurants, English menus and guides are increasing.

00:06:14.512 --> 00:06:16.767
<v Vocab>案内

00:06:17.168 --> 00:06:24.031
<v Host>Guide, information. You'll see this on signs everywhere. 'Annai-jo' means information desk.

00:06:24.632 --> 00:06:26.648
<v Host>And what the government says:

00:06:27.247 --> 00:06:36.800
<v Slow>日本政府は、もっと多くの外国人に来てほしいと言っています。

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:51.783
<v Host>The Japanese government says they want even more foreigners to come. 'Motto' means more, even more. 'Motto tabetai' means I want to eat more. 'Motto yukkuri' means more slowly. Very handy word.

00:06:52.384 --> 00:06:54.831
<v Vocab>てほしい

00:06:55.232 --> 00:07:01.927
<v Host>It means you want someone else to do something. Different from the 'tai' form, which is what you yourself want to do.

00:07:02.528 --> 00:07:07.783
<v Host>Let's listen to the full story one more time. You'll understand much more now.

00:07:08.384 --> 00:07:15.920
<v News>去年、日本に来た外国人の数が3000万人を超えました。

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:25.740
<v News>一番多い国は中国です。次は韓国、そしてアメリカです。

00:07:26.439 --> 00:07:34.192
<v News>外国人に人気がある場所は、東京、大阪、京都です。

00:07:34.891 --> 00:07:41.851
<v News>最近は、北海道や沖縄に行く人も増えています。

00:07:42.552 --> 00:07:49.752
<v News>外国人がたくさん来るので、ホテルの値段が高くなっています。

00:07:50.451 --> 00:08:02.572
<v News>日本語が分からなくて困る外国人もいます。駅やレストランで、英語のメニューや案内が増えています。

00:08:03.271 --> 00:08:11.120
<v News>日本政府は、「もっと多くの外国人に来てほしい」と言っています。

00:08:11.819 --> 00:08:14.555
<v Host>Quiz time. Three questions.

00:08:15.156 --> 00:08:22.283
<v Slow>去年、何人の外国人が日本に来ましたか？

00:08:23.084 --> 00:08:27.812
<v Host>How many foreigners came to Japan last year? Over thirty million.

00:08:28.411 --> 00:08:33.379
<v Slow>一番多い国はどこですか？

00:08:34.179 --> 00:08:37.635
<v Host>Which country has the most visitors? China.

00:08:38.235 --> 00:08:43.539
<v Slow>ホテルの値段はどうなっていますか？

00:08:44.340 --> 00:09:14.988
<v Host>What's happening to hotel prices? They're going up. Now let's do a vocabulary recap. 'Gaikokujin' means foreigner. 'Koemashita' means exceeded. 'Ninki ga aru' means popular. 'Saikin' means recently. 'Fuete imasu' means is increasing. 'Nedan' means price. 'Wakaranai' means don't understand. 'Komaru' means to have trouble. 'Annai' means guide or information. And 'motto' means more, even more.

00:09:15.587 --> 00:09:28.764
<v Host>That's the first episode of Japanese Daily News. Full transcript with furigana at podcast.jpnotes.dev. Subscribe on Apple Podcasts or Spotify. See you tomorrow.

00:09:29.364 --> 00:09:31.764
<v News>また明日。
